  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 956 PM MST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nDRY AND COOL THROUGH THE REST OF THE WEEKEND:\n\nThe axis of the trough that moved across our region today is now\neast of the Rocky Mountains, and drier air has moved into our\narea, ending precipitation. Dry weather is expected to persist \nthrough the remainder of the weekend while a weak upper-level \nridge moves across the Intermountain West. Clear skies are \nforecast through most of tonight, which will support cooler \nlow temperatures Sunday morning, including the first below \nfreezing lows for some desert valleys. \n\nQUICK MOVING WEATHER SYSTEM ON MONDAY: \n\nMoisture will begin to move back into the area Sunday night as a\nrelatively low-amplitude trough propagates across the West. This\ntrough and the associated lift is expected to quickly move \nacross our CWA on Monday. Moisture and forcing will be limited,\nso the current forecast calls for light showers and minimal snow\naccumulations over the higher terrain.   \n\nDRY AGAIN THROUGH MIDWEEK: \n\nBy Tuesday morning the trough will be east of our area, and\nridging aloft will build again. This upper-level ridging is \nexpected to bring dry weather and near or just below normal \ndaytime temperatures through at least midweek. Some \ndeterministic guidance suggests a weak disturbance passing \nduring the latter half of the workweek, but ensemble members\nstill show significant disagreement.",
